runtime changes: 0.4.41 → 0.4.43
## 0.4.43

- Default check and changed deploy show concrete witnessed questions; unwitnessed advisories stay behind `--review` and explicitly do not block. Actionable write/completeness notes remain visible. An acknowledgment without a replay witness states that limitation.
- Insert values equal to a declared literal default (including false/zero) initialize write-gated columns without the column grant; non-default inserts and changed updates still need it. Existing apps/stores keep their data; rebuild clients and restart servers to use the corrected rule.
- `serve-check` distinguishes missing stores, wrong script deployments, held locks and barrier failures, naming the resolved store and recorded holder. Readiness credentials remain private.
- `init --web --online --password` (or `--expo --online --password`) provides a small online password app, operator roles and working staging proofs, without guest/persona/todo fixtures. Password adaptation preserves web witness registration. Starter install helpers stream npm output with progress; React setup hints recognize existing starter wiring.
- Staging credential filenames are unique on each run. Account plans accept roleless `users` alongside provisioned roles; provisioning errors show redacted merged argument shapes and declared parameter names.
- Test anything against an app's npm serve script with `serve-check --then '<cmd>'` or `withServeScript`; startup, command deadlines, process-group shutdown and lock release are owned by the kit. Never write a serve driver. Readiness stays private; commands receive it through environment variables.
- Deploy, rollback and releases lead with verdict and live identity. Advisory decisions are counted; `--review` expands them and `--verbose` expands previews. JSON retains full evidence.
- `renderedText` scrolls unique candidates into view, including Expo web document scrolling. `devAccounts` provisions real development password users with the production helper's plan and return shape. `beginForm` accepts a subset of argument constraints, retaining exact checks on named values and trusted-click custody.
- Fitting extraction rollbacks prefer original source while preserving guards; previews and release JSON report `rowShape.matchesTarget` and concrete result differences. Live views print the columns/children they still serve. Reactivating identical source reuses a qualified retained release.
- Extraction admits safe list-bound widening; `deploy --save-version` can create its own configured baseline. Production roster locks record a renewable holder/lease and recover automatically when the kernel releases a dead holder.
- Shipping precision: SEC10 gates require a concrete current campaign witness; unsupported shapes and unresolved approval applicability remain advice behind `check --review`. Initial/edit audience differences, their projected-guard candidates, and migration-derived label equations are advisory. Witnessed acknowledgments reference the actual witness and operation, explicitly stating the intended creation audience or effect; source changes alone do not stale unchanged witnesses. A pending changed witness never reuses its old rationale.
- Security precision recognizes guarded workflow fields regardless of their names, recorded lifecycle effects, named approved media subjects, and text-list evidence. Required text minima reject zero-width, format and Unicode 17 default-ignorable characters; trim uses the same blank definition while retaining attached emoji presentation controls. Stored values that no longer satisfy a minimum refuse narrowing/adoption and need repair. Existing bytes remain readable; repair legacy format-only required values through an authorized mutation before editing their rows, or with the prior binary if adoption refuses. Stored text-list leaves retain their declared minimum and format constraints on adoption. Rebuild devices and restart servers; older executors do not enforce this hardening. Workflow admission includes Boolean wrappers and approving-principal receipts, while checked data remains approval evidence. Categorical self-assignments remain informational; parallel effects and renamed lifecycle fields retain their own contracts. Deploy retains deterministic work budgets with derived replay first and a smaller finite preflight; cuts remain unproved.
- Security precision: mutation edit-guard questions accept matching update-only intent, column write authority or an individual decision. Derived replay checks participant charge/refund conservation; current-input backfill labels ask an advisory meaning question, while deliberate no-refund removals take individual decisions. Null and values equal to a declared literal default initialize column write intent at insertion; changed edits retain authority checks. Unreviewed derived effects run first with one generated fixture per phase; an individual override cannot hide independent approval contracts. Deployment replay and final dispositions share one immutable decision snapshot.
- Ignored approval flags, timestamps and separately authorized approval records require an individual specification decision only when a current replay witnesses their modeled bypass and coupled applicability is established on the same row. External-act records are informational and do not gate deployment. Enforce the approval before its effect, or record why it is informational with `check --ack-security ID --reason '<spec rationale>'`.
- Derived security replay covers sibling approval paths, Boolean/enum/principal approval inputs, parent/child stages and repeatable creates. A production FIX names the exact candidate and its repair or individual alternative-path decision. Resource branch conditions retain their scope; unmappable caller conditions need explicit operation intent. Row aliases retain their target and source site; unresolved source targets and helper cuts report coverage gaps with the explicit-target or expression repair.
- New questions identify text-typed photo evidence, media beside private fields, one-sided anchored date windows, month-number arithmetic and global unique keys over owner-scoped rows. Empty-string justification guards that accept whitespace remain advice without a concrete witness; use `trim(value) != ''` or `text 1..N` on every supplied justification.
- Default check and production security campaigns use deterministic work budgets; machine load no longer selects a different security verdict. Search cuts retain explicit coverage gaps.
- PostgreSQL owners validate digest-bound flight-check contracts on open and reconnect. Missing or changed admitted helpers refuse instead of falling back; qualified sessions pin the durable namespace before temporary objects and require `array_nulls=on`. Older admitted helper versions are retained. These PostgreSQL checks do not change SQLite store format.

## 0.4.42

- Passing `check` is a compact verdict plus language enforcement facts; advisory decisions and notes are in `check --review` or JSON. Gates remain explicit and blocking.
- Security decisions carry redacted concrete campaign witnesses when available. Upsert questions name edits to existing rows. `check --ack-security` accepts repeated or comma-separated IDs with one rationale and commits them atomically; unrelated source edits preserve decisions.
- `usePage` includes `loading` and `why` alongside `read`. Developer refusal evidence names column write grants and caller-filtered hidden rows without changing public refusal disclosure.
- Schema-first React projects get a pinned starter hint. Expo starters include a web/native upload picker; upload overload errors explain sequential batches and Retry-After. Staging advances and redeploys print persisted clock offsets. Proof text is on-screen only; password starters include `probe:check` using `serve-check`.
- Existing apps retain their authored workflows; stores retain rows, accounts and migration receipts. Restart serve processes with 0.4.42 and rebuild generated clients/devices to use the new hook fields. Analyzer revision `sec11-1` makes older security decisions unreviewed once: inspect `check --review --json` and renew current IDs with a specification rationale. Default check no longer prints advisory details; gates and refutations remain blocking. SEC10 is deferred to 0.4.43.
History begins at 0.4.26; earlier changes are not recorded.
